{
  "id": "the_bench",
  "duration": 120,
  "fps": 24,
  "hook_end": 2.5,
  "narration": "Every morning on my way to school, I passed the same park bench. An old man sat there, a brown paper bag in his lap. Pigeons crowded around his shoes, waiting for the crumbs he scattered. I always looked at him, but he never looked back. So one morning I brought a paper bag of my own. I stopped beside the bench and dropped crumbs onto the path. For a moment, nothing happened; I thought he hadn't noticed me. Then he lifted his head, looked at me, and nodded. After that, I came back every morning before school. Two brown bags, one wooden bench, not a single word between us. He showed me how to hold my hand still for a pigeon. When one finally landed and ate, I almost laughed, but stayed still. One morning I reached the park, and the bench was empty. There was no paper bag, no one feeding the birds. His flat cap was sitting right where he used to sit. I picked it up carefully, and kept it safe for him. Days went by, and every morning I found that empty seat. I started sitting there myself, his cap resting beside me. The pigeons still came for breakfast, so I brought crumbs for them. I kept looking down the path, hoping he'd appear around the corner. Then one grey morning, a shadow fell across the bench beside me. It was him, moving more slowly, his coat loose, leaning on a cane. He sat beside me; I handed him his cap, and he put it on. Then he pulled out two paper bags, and handed one to me again.",
